      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14228914/assets/9ceef67f02c022065be31de7ec1b6785d270c194aee29c4d0a20707998932134.png)\n\nGiven the triangle above, find the length of $\\overset{―}{BC}$ rounded to one decimal place.",
      "worked_solution": "For an angle $\\theta $ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}}$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the length of the hypotenuse $\\overset{―}{AB}$ and wish to find the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{BC}$ adjacent to the given angle $∠B.$\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the adjacent leg to the hypotenuse is *cosine*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(m∠B) & =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡({38}^{∘}) & =\\frac{BC}{12} \\\\ BC & =12\\operatorname{cos}⁡({38}^{∘}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =12\\operatorname{cos}⁡({38}^{∘}) \\\\  & =12(0.788\\,01…) \\\\  & =9.456\\,12… \\\\  & ≈9.5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.",
